---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { FACTS as F, n } from '../lib/facts';
const repo = 'https://github.com/binjesoft-ui/datalle';
const studies = [
  {
    id: 'schema-drift',
    title: 'The source files do not agree with each other',
    problem: `The TLC publishes one Parquet file per month, and the files are not consistent. The same column can have a
      different type in different months (passenger count is a whole number in some files and a decimal in others), and
      column names change case (Airport_fee vs airport_fee). Reading all twelve months together fails or silently
      produces nulls.`,
    approach: [
      'Bronze casts every file to one fixed list of 19 columns and types, matching names without regard to case.',
      'A missing column becomes an empty typed column instead of an error, so a new or dropped field never breaks a load.',
      'Each month is written with replaceWhere, so re-running a month replaces it instead of adding duplicates.',
      'Every row keeps its source month, file name and load time, so any number can be traced back to a file.',
    ],
    result: `All 12 months load into one table of ${n(F.bronzeRows)} rows. Tests build deliberately inconsistent files
      (mixed types, mixed casing, duplicates) and check that they load and clean correctly.`,
    skills: ['Schema enforcement', 'Idempotent loads', 'Lineage columns'],
  },
  {
    id: 'quarantine',
    title: `${F.quarantinePct}% of trips are invalid: delete them or keep them?`,
    problem: `Raw taxi data contains trips that end before they start, last more than six hours, travel at impossible speeds
      or have zero or negative fares. Deleting them silently would make the published totals impossible to check, and would
      hide how much was removed.`,
    approach: [
      `${F.rulesCount} rules written as plain SQL expressions, each true for a valid trip. A missing value counts as a failure.`,
      'Rows that fail any rule go to a quarantine table with the names of every rule they broke.',
      'The rules live in one list that drives cleaning, the quality report and the tests.',
    ],
    result: `${n(F.evaluatedRows)} unique rows were checked: ${n(F.cleanRows)} clean and ${n(F.quarantinedRows)}
      quarantined (${F.quarantinePct}%), plus ${F.duplicatesRemoved} exact duplicates removed. Clean + quarantined +
      duplicates equals the raw count exactly. The largest cause was passenger count:
      ${n(F.passengerFailed)} trips (${F.passengerPct}%) record zero or no passengers.`,
    lesson: `That passenger rule is a judgement call. Many of those trips are probably real journeys where the driver
      did not enter a count. Because they are quarantined and not deleted, the rule can be relaxed later and the
      trips restored without reloading anything.`,
    skills: ['Data quality rules', 'Quarantine pattern', 'Reconciliation'],
  },
  {
    id: 'two-implementations',
    title: 'Two implementations, one answer',
    problem: `The same cleaning logic was rebuilt as a Lakeflow declarative pipeline, where the rules become expectations
      that Databricks enforces and reports. Two versions of the same rules can easily drift apart.`,
    approach: [
      'The pipeline file keeps its own copy of the rules, and a test fails if it ever differs from the notebook list.',
      'The pipeline table code is run locally with the Databricks decorators stubbed out, and compared row by row with the notebook result.',
      'After a real run, the pass and fail counts from the pipeline event log are compared with the notebook quality report.',
    ],
    result: `All ${F.rulesCount} rules failed exactly the same number of rows in both versions, over the same
      ${n(F.evaluatedRows)} rows. A test in the site build now fails if they ever disagree.`,
    lesson: `The local comparison caught a real bug before it reached Databricks. Expectations drop bad rows only after
      the table query has run, so a $0 fare reached the tip-percentage division and would have stopped the pipeline with
      a divide-by-zero error. Using try_divide fixed it.`,
    skills: ['Lakeflow declarative pipelines', 'Expectations', 'Testing Databricks code locally'],
  },
  {
    id: 'no-server',
    title: '35 million trips on a free static site',
    problem: `The dashboard should let anyone filter a year of trips by date, borough and payment type and see exact
      averages, without paying for a server or keeping the Databricks workspace running.`,
    approach: [
      'Three gold cubes store only sums and counts for every combination of the filter dimensions, never averages.',
      'They are exported as compressed Parquet files and served as plain static files.',
      'The browser reads them with a small Parquet library, adds up the rows that match the filters, and only then divides, so every average is exact for the selection.',
      'Tests check that all three cubes give identical trip totals under many filter combinations.',
    ],
    result: `${n(F.cleanRows)} clean trips are served as ${n(F.cubeRows)} cube rows in ${F.cubeMB} MB. Filters respond
      instantly, hosting is free, and the site works even when Databricks is idle.`,
    lesson: `The first deploy showed "could not load data" because a *.parquet rule in .gitignore kept the cube files out
      of the repository. A specific exception for the site's data folder fixed it, and the files are now checked by tests.`,
    skills: ['Data modelling for serving', 'Additive measures', 'Static data delivery'],
  },
];
---
<Base title="Case studies">
  <h1>Case studies</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Four real problems met while building this project, each written as problem, approach and result. Every number on
    this page is checked by a test against the published data files, so it cannot go out of date silently.
  </p>
  <nav class="toc" aria-label="Case studies">
    {studies.map((s, i) => <a href={`#${s.id}`}>{i + 1}. {s.title}</a>)}
  </nav>

  {studies.map((s, i) => (
    <article class="card study" id={s.id}>
      <p class="num-label">Case study {i + 1}</p>
      <h2>{s.title}</h2>
      <h3>Problem</h3>
      <p>{s.problem}</p>
      <h3>Approach</h3>
      <ul>{s.approach.map((a) => <li>{a}</li>)}</ul>
      <h3>Result</h3>
      <p>{s.result}</p>
      {s.lesson && (<><h3>Lesson</h3><p>{s.lesson}</p></>)}
      <p class="skills">{s.skills.map((k) => <span>{k}</span>)}</p>
    </article>
  ))}

  <p class="lede small">
    See the <a href="/architecture/">Architecture</a> page for how the parts fit together, and the code at
    <a href={repo} rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .toc { display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:.3rem; margin:1rem 0 1.5rem; }
  .toc a { color:var(--accent); text-decoration:none; }
  .toc a:hover { text-decoration:underline; }
  .study { margin:1.25rem 0; padding:1.25rem 1.5rem; scroll-margin-top:1rem; }
  .study h2 { margin:.1rem 0 .75rem; }
  .study h3 { margin:1rem 0 .25rem; font-size:.8rem; text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.08em; color:var(--muted); }
  .study p, .study ul { margin:.25rem 0; }
  .num-label { color:var(--accent); font-size:.8rem; font-weight:600; margin:0; }
  .skills { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:.4rem; margin-top:1rem !important; }
  .skills span { font-size:.75rem; padding:.15rem .55rem; border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:999px; color:var(--muted); }
  .small { font-size:.85rem; }
</style>
